cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this work, please cite it as below."
title: "AnaPlex: An Architecture for Real-Time Learning"
abstract: >-
  Transformers cannot learn in real time because their weights do not
  change during inference. AnaPlex proposes freezing the computational units
  and making only the wiring plastic. The design is derived from three
  asymmetries in biological neural systems rather than from combining
  existing machine learning architectures: the genome cannot specify
  synaptic weights (six orders of magnitude short), the cortex is
  uniform so modality is determined by connectivity rather than by the
  units themselves, and memory resides in synapses rather than neurons
  at a ratio of 1:1000. Learning is driven by prediction error alone,
  with no reward function.
